/**
 * What a program asks about the machine it runs on (docs/design/OS.md): the environment it was started with, which
 * system and version it is, and where the user's files belong. The three target constants of `std/core` are
 * re-exported, so that a file that branches on the system writes one import.
 *
 * - [Environment] and [EnvironmentVariables] - read one variable, list them all, split the search path.
 * - [System] and [SystemVersion] - the version, the host name, the uptime, the page size, the machine's architecture.
 * - [Directories] - home, configuration, data, state, cache and temporary files, by the convention of the system.
 * - [Entropy] - bytes of the system's source of randomness, for keys, nonces and the seed of a generator.
 * - [OsError] - what every question that can fail fails with.
 *
 * A branch on the system is a `match OperatingSystem.current`: every arm is type checked on every machine, and only the
 * arm of the build's target is compiled. Each module above is such a branch, and each arm hands its question to a
 * directory of its own - `windows/`, `linux/`, `macos/`, `freebsd/`, `browser/` for one system, `posix/`, `bsd/`,
 * `xdg/` for a standard several share. Those directories hold the raw natives (`browser/` has none: a page asks the
 * machine nothing); `torb check --every-target` reports one that is reached on a system it does not exist on.
 *
 * # Examples
 *
 *     fn pathListSeparator(): String {
 *       match OperatingSystem.current {
 *         .Windows => ";"
 *         .Linux | .MacOs | .FreeBsd | .Browser => ":"
 *       }
 *     }
 *
 * # Related
 *
 * - [OperatingSystem] - the constant a branch reads, with the cases every branch handles.
 */

public use OperatingSystem, Architecture, ByteOrder from "std/core"
public use OsError from "./error"
public use Environment, EnvironmentVariables from "./environment"
public use System, SystemVersion from "./system"
public use Directories from "./directories"
public use Entropy from "./entropy"
